The expected outputs come from a hand-worked reduction, not from running a program. Here f(x, y) is the bottleneck distance, i.e. the largest edge on the MST path between x and y. Processing MST edges in increasing weight, each vertex carries a signed count (+1 per occurrence in A, −1 per occurrence in B). When two components with opposite-sign leftovers merge at weight w, they pay w × min(|s1|, |s2|). The sum of these costs is the answer. I checked this by hand against both samples (8 and 3) before computing the ten cases below.

